              "B": "Research fused permutation generation and record maintenance. Maintain one formatted record with immutable punctuation and spaces; manipulate only its symbol positions during pivot search, successor swap and suffix reversal. Alternatively maintain contiguous symbols and update only changed record positions. Compare both against serial std::next_permutation plus full record formatting using the same buffered writer, isolating generator improvements from I/O improvements. Preserve the source's character comparison order and duplicate handling. Explicitly bound terminal suffix searches to avoid before-begin access or index underflow. Keep generation and emission separable for integration; copying output bytes remains unavoidable.",
              "C": "Investigate ordered parallel generation on at most four total CPU threads, only through facilities supported by the unchanged build. Partition the multiset permutation space into a lexicographically ordered, prefix-free frontier of disjoint prefix subtrees. Each producer enumerates its remaining suffix; one ordered writer emits bounded chunks in subtree order. Bound pending tasks and buffers, and reserve progress for the earliest unfinished subtree so later tasks cannot cause deadlock. Do not materialize all permutations or use overflowing factorial counts. Retain a serial fallback based on general work estimates, especially for duplicate-heavy or small-output inputs. Measure whether generation parallelism actually outweighs coordination and output contention; abandoning this approach is valid.",
              "G": "Validate and measure each tree's complete program independently under the existing harness, compiler, target and budgets. Compare A and B individually, then their combination; evaluate C with the strongest compatible serial engine only if resources permit. Integration gains are not additive, and additional threads may hurt. Keep the fastest validated complete candidate, including a standalone tree result if merges regress. Check exact formatting, character ordering, duplicate suppression, empty and singleton behavior, final-buffer draining, oversized records and parallel ordering. Use existing repeated measurements and the real output path; treat small differences cautiously. Do not change tests or the oracle, specialize to test strings, or substitute precomputed answers.",
              "analysis": "The relevant task is Problem D, not the other contest problems. The only measured program is the correct baseline: mean 0.140974 seconds, with three samples spanning 0.137973–0.143810 seconds and four correctness cases. No optimization gains or bottleneck profiles are available. Source inspection strongly suggests an output-overhead hypothesis: every permutation uses multiple formatted insertions and std::endl forces a flush. For nonempty length n and P distinct multiset permutations, the required output is P(2n+4) bytes, so those bytes cannot be eliminated. Prioritize buffering, then generation/formatting fusion; treat four-core parallelism as higher risk on this short workload. All trees must read all stdin, remove exactly the six whitespace characters recognized by the source, emit every distinct permutation in source character order, and produce no output for an empty filtered input. Preserve correctness for every valid input, not merely the four observed cases."
