              "B": "Research input/output overhead independently. Compare fscanf with buffered input and correctly rounded conversion such as strtod, preserving accepted numeric syntax, whitespace, token boundaries, EOF handling, and unrestricted double values. Retain the existing column-major compute layout initially. Then evaluate accumulating arithmetic sums and reciprocal sums while parsing: each column must receive additions in precisely its original row order, with unchanged division timing. Avoid approximate decimal conversion, restricted numeric ranges, or reassociated reductions. Buffer output only while retaining libc formatting, seven metric lines, one decimal place, and trailing spaces. Separate parsing gains from accumulation-fusion gains in the available measurements.",
              "C": "Research column-level execution and kernel fusion. Keep each column's complete statistical computation together, with original-order mean/harmonic reductions, sorting, then sorted-order variance. If the unchanged build already supports threading, evaluate one coarse-grained region using at most four column workers, private sorting scratch, and disjoint results; do not introduce compiler or build-setting changes. Compare serial, two-worker, and four-worker execution within the existing budget, retaining a dimension/work-based serial fallback because the baseline is short. Otherwise pursue the serial fused kernel and reduced allocation overhead. Do not parallelize within-column reductions, substitute the cancellation-prone sum-of-squares variance formula, or assume pow is an expensive call without evidence.",
