            "B": "Research exact visited-state storage and allocation, keeping successor legality unchanged. Begin with the inexpensive cached-hash comparison before memcmp in lookup. Then evaluate deterministic hash mixing for bucket selection and an open-addressed table holding stable state indices, always resolving collisions with complete player-and-box key equality. Construct candidate keys in reusable scratch storage and allocate permanent records only for first visits. Evaluate a correctly aligned arena with compact parent handles and separate frontier vectors, avoiding intrusive chaining and queue pointers. Arena growth must not invalidate references. Frontier vectors must reproduce the current reverse-discovery layer traversal, including immediate goal checks and first-discovery parent retention; ordinary FIFO replacement is not equivalent. Benchmark hash filtering, table replacement, and compact storage independently before combining them.",
            "C": "Research sound deadlock pruning without replacing unit-move BFS. Start with a cheap local check after pushes: a completely wall/box-filled 2x2 block containing an off-goal box is permanently frozen. Next evaluate per-goal reverse-push reachability, including the required player-support square, extending the existing union-valued live map. Build the necessary box-to-goal compatibility graph and reject configurations lacking an injective assignment of every box to a distinct reachable goal. Do not assume equal box and goal counts. Evaluate initially and after pushes; ordinary walks can reuse the box-configuration result. Any cache must verify exact sorted box keys. Preserve surviving-state order and never reject solved configurations. Avoid speculative deadlock rules, push-count optimization, or player-region merging. Retain only pruning variants whose reduced search outweighs their preprocessing, matching, and cache costs.",
