          "plan": {
            "A": "Refine bc5d99c2c37544e1's fused row-first streaming algorithm. Preserve its min(s,n-s)-row history and O(n²) work. Target unnecessary ring writes: save a row's horizontal sums only when row+s<n, and stop overwriting horizontal-ring entries once they cannot become outgoing values again. Separate initialization, reusable-history and final-consumption phases so these decisions remain outside hot loops. Measure each change independently against the existing A program. Preserve 64-bit arithmetic, subtraction-before-overwrite and evaluation only after a complete s-row window exists.",
            "B": "Resolve the input/SIMD confound before extending NEON tuning. Starting from 99401c1685db48d4, first replace scanf with A's bounded fread parser while leaving the packed-window kernel unchanged. Measure that controlled variant. Separately investigate a pointer/end parser hot path with a safe refill path for tokens crossing buffer boundaries; evaluate it on A's unchanged kernel. Support signed, variable-length integers, arbitrary valid whitespace and INT_MIN without fixed-width assumptions. Retain packed-row NEON or unrolling only when end-to-end measurements justify it. SIMD lanes must represent independent windows; never assert independence of successive rolling-sum iterations.",
            "C": "Use 515821c6b1c44bc7 as the parallel reference, not an assumed improvement over A. Prioritize bounded 1-, 2- and 4-thread configurations within the existing budget, including a truly serial path without team startup. Investigate band count independently of worker count, accounting for cooperative seed work, overlap reuse, n-s+1 valid starting rows and serial parsing. Preserve aligned private states, static ownership and one final maximum reduction. The 64-row batched program is a secondary reference: pursue coarser batching only if reduced synchronization outweighs staging traffic. Require an end-to-end improvement, not merely a faster compute loop.",
            "G": "Anchor integration on bc5d99c2c37544e1 and retain that complete program as the fallback. Evaluate child winners independently, then combine compatible improvements one at a time—for example, a proven parser improvement with A's proven lifetime optimization. Treat streaming, packed-row SIMD and full-matrix parallel traversal as alternative backends rather than mechanically merging their storage and loops. Measure every actual combination under the unchanged evaluator; improvements are not additive. Introduce dispatch only with a general geometry/work-cost justification and measured benefit, never test identities or matrix contents. Audit negative-only inputs, s=1, s=n-1, ring wraparound, complete-window coverage and widened subtraction. Select the fastest correct measured complete program, including unchanged A if integration regresses.",
            "analysis": "Only Problem B is relevant: maximum-sum s×s submatrix for 0<s<n<8000. The baseline rescans edges in O(s n²); the supplied replacements perform O(n²) work. Best A, bc5d99c2c37544e1, measures 9.129 ms versus 126.056 ms baseline, about 13.8× faster. Other A/C programs measure 9.664–10.026 ms, so parallelism has not demonstrated a win. B measures about 54 ms, but its shown lineage retains scanf; this does not isolate NEON's contribution. No phase profiles or optimized-run variance are supplied. Prioritize lifetime/traffic reduction, controlled parser experiments and parallel-overhead control. Across all roles, preserve signed 64-bit intermediates despite the 32-bit output guarantee, all-valid-input correctness, and the existing compiler, target, tests, oracle and budgets."
          },
